I do have issues with this game (see below), but (bearing in mind I haven't completed story mode yet) I will first say this: The Lego Movie Videogame is

* It's funny as a spoof of the hero quests commonly found in fantasy games and novels;
* Most of the dialogue is amusing, if not downright hilarious;
* The co-op "puzzles" are superbly done and have a smooth complexity curve so you don't lose interest but aren't out of your depth;
* It has plenty of minigames, including a few car(t) chases that are all unique enough to keep your interest;
* The voice acting is amusing in and of itself: Morgan Freeman plays a very sarcastic blind/is-he-really-blind man, and I'm fairly convinced that Liam Neeson plays Bad/Good-but-kinda-crazy Cop. Whoever does the voice of Batman plays up his angst in well-timed and often hilarious over-dramatisation;
* It's generally entertaining. It's wacky, weird and wonderful. I spent most of the time grinning because the characters (and plot) are all so ridiculously charming and funny.
* There's no tutorial to teach you the controls. You get hints pop up from time to time, but they're kind of random and very location-specific: if you're not standing right on an activation circle, you'll miss the tooltip telling you which key to press to save the day. I did think this was a problem with the demo (since I've only ever played demos before and they've all had the same lack of tutorial), but the full game doesn't make any real attempt at teaching you how to move or which keys do what. I restarted the game a few times because I couldn't figure out how to "Continue" out of the summary screen and thought it had frozen up;
* You can't reset the controls to suit you, and the default map just isn't that intuitive. In some instances, you skip cut-scenes or intro screens with U, but in others it's F2. Sometimes, you can exit with ESC, other times you need BACKSPACE. To "continue" out of a summary screen, you need to press J. Some attacks are interchangable with interactions (eg. Batman can use H
K to grapple some things, even though H is generally for attack and K generally for interaction)... The controls are already hard to remember because they're not intuitive (or commonly used), but when you add this interchangability, it becomes even more confusing.
I may add more as I continue playing, but the pros so far outweigh the cons and I would recommend this game for anyone who wants to have a laugh while playing a game that doesn't take itself seriously. :D

Talking about this game I can't but look back to the Neverhood, as it has been the symbol of using clay in gamedev so far. But not anymore! Meet a new claymotion  leader - Don't open the doors!
This game has been made by a single person! Really, just think about that, guys! Such a huge, colourful and funny world was made by one single man! Isn't that freaking awesome?! Well, even is it had been made by a bloody army of developers, it's great anyway.
Ok, emotions aside, what I like about this game most of all:
+ Tasty clay world;
+ Living environment: everything is moving, talking and can be smashed;
+ Good humour;
+ Exciting quests and hardcore challenges.
What I don't like:
- It's not easy to move diagonally using keyboard, but I guess it's an inevitable problem is case of wasd controls;
SUMMING UP: Don't you even dare to doubt - just buy the game, try it and fall in love with it! Besides, the price is very attractive!

